    """Anchor every continued hop's newly rotated identities.

    The hop records chain the pair boundaries' cumulative entry
    counts, so one continued hop rotates exactly the identities
    whose global ledger position lies in the window between the
    previous pair boundary and its own. The last proof remembers
    the whole prefix up to the divergence point of a terminal
    failure as one ordered identity list -- its authenticated
    prefix evidence followed by its flattened retained entries --
    so every window up to that point is reconstructed exactly:
    rotated must equal the window's identities restricted to the
    requested set, in request order. Windows are cumulative and
    disjoint, and a rotated identity can never reappear later.
    Only a window running past everything the last (older or
    divergent) proof remembers can be anchored just at its known
    edge; that case is confined to a terminal non-fork failure.
    Any contradiction raises :class:`ValueError`."""
